nny lines exists\n\nAssume three distinct lines \\(L,M_{1},M_{2}\\) cover all points of \\(S\\) and that exactly two of them are sunny.  \nConsequently one line, say \\(L\\), is non\u2011sunny (of the form (2)), while \\(M_{1},M_{2}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(|L\\cap S|=3\\)\n\nIf \\(L\\) contains three points of \\(S\\), then by (3) \\(L\\) is one of \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).\n\n*If \\(L=L_{1}\\;(y=1)\\):*  \n\\(S\\setminus L=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nThe three lines determined by any two of these points are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,2)-(1,3):\\;x=1\\quad\\text{(vertical, non\u2011sunny)},\\\\\n&(1,2)-(2,2):\\;y=2\\quad\\text{(horizontal, non\u2011sunny)},\\\\\n&(1,3)-(2,2):\\;x+y=4\\quad\\text{(slope }-1\\text{, non\u2011sunny)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, so two sunny lines cannot cover them \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L=L_{2}\\;(x=1)\\):*  \n\\(S\\setminus L=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAgain the three possible lines through two of these points are respectively horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\); all are non\u2011sunny, giving the same contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L=L_{3}\\;(x+y=4)\\):*  \n\\(S\\setminus L=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe lines through any two of these points are \\(x=1\\) (vertical), \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) and \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), all non\u2011sunny. Contradiction again.\n\nHence \\(|L\\cap S|=3\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(|L\\cap S|=2\\)\n\nFrom the enumeration above the non\u2011sunny lines that contain exactly two points of \\(S\\) are  \n\n\\[\ny=2,\\qquad x=2,\\qquad x+y=3 .\n\\]\n\nWe treat each separately.\n\n*If \\(L:y=2\\):*  \n\\(S\\setminus L=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe six lines through any two of these points are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(2,1):\\;y=1,\\\\\n&(