_n\\) and containing at least one sunny line.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{\\,i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid x=i\\text{ belongs to }\\mathcal{L}\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of indices of vertical lines present in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).  \nDenote by \\(\\mathcal{N}=\\mathcal{L}\\setminus\\{x=i\\mid i\\in V\\}\\) the set of **non‑vertical** lines; \\(|\\mathcal{N}|=n-|V|\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor any column \\(C_i\\) with \\(i\\notin V\\) the points of \\(C_i\\) can be covered only by lines of \\(\\mathcal{N}\\), and each line of \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) meets \\(C_i\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is not vertical, its equation is either \\(y=c\\), \\(x+y=c\\), or has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). In all cases two distinct points of the same column would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical, a contradiction. ∎\n\nConsequently, for every \\(i\\notin V\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{N}|\\;\\ge\\;|C_i|=n+1-i. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Induction)  \n\\(V=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); i.e. **all** vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(i\\).*\n\n*Base \\(i=1\\).*  \nColumn \\(C_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points. If \\(1\\notin V\\), then by (1) we would need \\(|\\mathcal{N}|\\ge n\\). Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), this would force \\(\\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{N}\\) and leave **no** line to cover any other column \\(C_j\\) (\\(j\\ge2\\)), which is impossible because those columns are non‑empty. Hence \\(1\\in V\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume \\(1,2,\\dots ,k-1\\in V\\) for some \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nAfter fixing these \\(k-1\\) vertical lines we have \\(n-(k-1)\\) lines left.  \nIf \\(k\\notin V\\), then by (1) we need at least \\(|C_k|=n+1-k\\) non‑vertical lines to cover \\(C_k\\). Hence the number of lines remaining for the later columns is at most  \n\n\\[\nn-(k-1)-(n+1-k)=k-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut column \\(C_{k+1}\\) contains \\(n-k\\) points, and for every \\(k\\