 passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) of the last column \\(C_n\\).  \nAll other points of \\(S_n\\) lie in columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and are already covered by the corresponding vertical lines.  \nHence we obtain a covering with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing at least two sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{\\,i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid x=i\\in\\mathcal L\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of columns whose vertical line is present, and let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{x=i\\mid i\\in V\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of non‑vertical lines.  \nThus \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-|V|\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|\\ge 2\\) because at least two sunny lines belong to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\nFor any column \\(i\\notin V\\) the points of \\(C_i\\) must be supplied by lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nSince each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) meets column \\(i\\) in at most one point, we have\n\n\\[\n|C_i|\\le |\\mathcal N|\\qquad\\text{for every }i\\notin V. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|C_i|>2\\) then (1) forces \\(i\\in V\\).  \nNow \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\); the inequality \\(|C_i|>2\\) is equivalent to \\(i\\le n-2\\).  \nHence **all columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) are vertical**, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\subseteq V . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|V|\\ge n-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad |\\mathcal N|=n-|V|\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause we assumed at least two sunny lines, we must have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|=2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe only columns that may lack a vertical line are \\(n-1\\) (size \\(2\\)) and \\(n\\) (size \\(1\\)).  \nWith exactly two non‑vertical lines we can cover at most two points of column \\(n-1\\) (one per line) and at most two points of column \\(n\\) (again one per line).\n\nTo cover column \\(n\\) we need one of the two lines to pass through its unique point \\((n,1)\\).  \nThe other line must then pass through the second point of column \\(n-1\\), namely \\((n-1,2)\\).\n\nConsider the line that contains \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\). Its slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-2}{n-(n-1)}=-1,\n\\]\n\nso the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and **is not sunny**.  \nIf instead the line through \\((n,1)\\) also passes through \\((n-1,1)\\), its slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), again not sunny.  \nThus no sunny line can simultaneously pass through \\((n,1)\\) and a point of column \\(n-1\\) distinct from \\((n-1,1)\\).\n\nHence it is impossible to cover the points of columns \\(n-1\\) and \\(n\\) with two distinct sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that such a covering exists.\n\nTherefore a covering of \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain **at most one** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n- Configurations with \\(k=0\\) (all vertical lines) exist.\n- Configurations with \\(k=1\\) (vertical lines for columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) plus the sunny line \\(y=x-(n-1)\\)) exist.\n- No configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
