athcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\).  \nWe consider every possible non‑sunny line that contains at least one point of \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny line of the form \\(x=c\\) (vertical)\n\nThe only values of \\(c\\) that meet a point of \\(P\\) are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n| \\(c\\) | points on \\(x=c\\) | uncovered points \\(U\\) |\n|------|-------------------|------------------------|\n| 1    | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\(\\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\) |\n| 2    | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)       | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\) |\n| 3    | \\((3,1)\\)             | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\) |\n\nFor each set \\(U\\) we show that two sunny lines cannot cover it.\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  Any line through two points of \\(U\\) has one of the slopes  \n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(2,1)-(3,1):\\;0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\\\\n  &(2,1)-(2,2):\\;\\infty\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\\\\n  &(3,1)-(2,2):\\;-1 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  All three slopes are forbidden, so a sunny line can contain **at most one**\n  point of \\(U\\).  With only two sunny lines we could cover at most two points,\n  but \\(|U|=3\\).  Impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  The unordered pairs of points of \\(U\\) and their slopes are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(1,2) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n  (1,2)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n  (1,3)-(3,1) & -1\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  The only *sunny* slope is \\(-\\tfrac12\\), occurring for the pair\n  \\((1,2),(3,1)\\).  Hence a sunny line can contain at most the two points\n  \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); the remaining two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\)\n  would each need a separate sunny line, contradicting the fact that we have\n  only two sunny lines in total.  Impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  The uncovered set is \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\)