            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n:=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\text{h}}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\text{v}}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\text{d}}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\nDenote by \\(S\\) the set of sunny lines and by \\(N\\) the set of non‑sunny lines; \\(|S|=k,\\ |N|=n-k\\).\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) it is non‑sunny, so a sunny line has a different rational slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(0,1),(1,0),(1,-1)\\).  \nMoving from one lattice point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) to the next along the line changes the coordinates by \\((q,p)\\).  \nConsequently the number of lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on the line is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = M .\n\\]\n\nThe only slope for which the bound can be attained is \\(p=q=1\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)), which indeed meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\). ∎\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\) and contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points; the same holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains at most \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,2n+2-c\\}\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\nHence the total number of points covered by the configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nN_n\\le kM+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit value of \\(M\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) (even)*, then \\(M=m\\) and (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\nm(2m+1)\\le km+(n-k)n = km+2m(2m-k) .\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\(m\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2m+1\\le 4m-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 2m-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{2a}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd)*, then \\(M=m+1\\) and (1) reads  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)\\le k(m+1)+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n=2m+1\\),\n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)\\le k(m+1)+(2m+1)(2m+1-k)\n      = (2m+1)^2 -k m .\n\\]\n\nRearranging yields  \n\n\\[\nk m\\le (2m+1)m\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 2m+1=n-2 .\n\\tag{2b}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\nn-2,& n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that the bounds in (3) are sharp.\n\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the \\(y\\)-coordinate of any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n#### 2.2  Adding sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le n\\).  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,s\\) choose a slope \\(\\alpha_j\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and distinct from the other chosen slopes (for instance \\(\\alpha_j=j\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y-\\;j = \\alpha_j\\,(x-1) .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) passes through the point \\((1,j)\\) and, because its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), it is sunny.  \nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_s\\) are pairwise distinct (different slopes) and none of them coincides with any of the horizontal lines \\(y=i\\).\n\nNow keep the horizontal lines \\(y=s+1,\\dots ,y=n\\) and discard the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=s\\).  \nThe resulting family consists of\n\n* the \\(s\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_s\\);\n* the \\(n-s\\) horizontal lines \\(y=s+1,\\dots ,y=n\\).\n\nIt contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered:\n\n* if \\(b\\le s\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_b\\) (because all \\(L_b\\) contain the whole vertical line \\(x=1\\) at height \\(b\\) and, being non‑vertical, intersect the vertical line \\(x=a\\) at a unique point, which is precisely \\((a,b)\\));\n* if \\(b\\ge s+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nThus we have produced, for **every** integer \\(s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le n\\), a configuration with exactly \\(k=s\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.3  Respecting the upper bound\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even, the construction works for all \\(s\\le n-1\\); the case \\(s=n\\) would give \\(k=n\\) sunny lines, but (2a) shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible, so the maximal attainable value is \\(n-1\\).\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, the construction works for all \\(s\\le n-2\\); the case \\(s=n-1\\) would give \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines, which contradicts (2b). Hence the maximal attainable value is \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (3) with the explicit constructions of §2 we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd:  } &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nEvery integer in the indicated set can be realised by a suitable choice of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and no other integer can occur. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
